WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.640
How can the edge of a growth cone move forward while its actin network streams

2
00:00:07.640 --> 00:00:08.640
backward?

3
00:00:09.183 --> 00:00:10.503
Look inside the fan.

4
00:00:11.543 --> 00:00:13.763
New actin joins the filaments at the front edge.

5
00:00:14.733 --> 00:00:17.713
Myosin II pulls the network back toward the centre.

6
00:00:18.653 --> 00:00:20.473
Its own assembly pushes it back too.

7
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.087
Near the centre, the filaments are taken apart.

8
00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:27.527
This backward movement is called retrograde flow.

9
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:34.390
In 1988, Paul Forscher and Stephen Smith filmed this in a sea slug with giant, flat

10
00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.390
growth cones.

11
00:00:36.133 --> 00:00:40.553
They added cytochalasin, a drug that stops actin adding at the front edge.

12
00:00:41.533 --> 00:00:43.033
Addition at the front stops.

13
00:00:43.993 --> 00:00:46.173
What happens to the actin just behind the edge?

14
00:00:52.133 --> 00:00:54.793
An empty band opens at the front and widens.

15
00:00:55.613 --> 00:00:57.433
The rest of the network keeps moving back.

16
00:00:58.367 --> 00:01:00.067
So the flow was there all along.

17
00:01:01.047 --> 00:01:02.907
Addition at the front had been hiding it.

18
00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:06.800
Then the microtubules push forward into the emptied band.

19
00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.340
The actin had been holding them back.

20
00:01:17.300 --> 00:01:19.140
So why does the edge ever advance?

21
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.700
In 1988, Tim Mitchison and Marc Kirschner proposed a clutch.

22
00:01:24.733 --> 00:01:28.893
Think of the actin network as a running engine, and the ground as the road.

23
00:01:29.917 --> 00:01:34.817
If actin is added and carried back at the same rate, the engine runs, and the edge

24
00:01:34.817 --> 00:01:35.817
goes nowhere.

25
00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.740
Adhesion molecules can link the network to the ground.

26
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.120
Then the clutch engages, and the backward flow slows.

27
00:01:44.233 --> 00:01:47.553
If building stays the same, it now pushes the edge forward.

28
00:01:50.367 --> 00:01:51.707
Put it as bookkeeping.

29
00:01:52.807 --> 00:01:56.387
Advance equals actin added at the edge, minus actin carried back.

30
00:01:57.350 --> 00:01:59.550
Suter and colleagues made the grip themselves.

31
00:02:00.610 --> 00:02:03.930
They held a bead coated with an adhesion molecule against the edge.

32
00:02:04.933 --> 00:02:07.273
Behind the bead, the backward flow slowed.

33
00:02:09.083 --> 00:02:13.703
With illustrative numbers, flow falls from three and a half to half a micrometre a

34
00:02:13.703 --> 00:02:18.163
minute. Building stays the same, so the edge moves seven times faster.

35
00:02:20.383 --> 00:02:24.623
Now make the grip, or the building, unequal across the two sides of the cone.

36
00:02:26.267 --> 00:02:31.747
Carry one idea. Grip can speed the edge just by holding back the flow, with no

37
00:02:31.747 --> 00:02:32.487
faster building.
